Standard Array vs. ArrayList

                                                         ********** Declaration *********
• Primitive Variable: int num = 10;
• Standard Array (Empty with Size): int[] arr = new int[5];
• Standard Array (With Values): int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
• ArrayList (Empty): ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();
• ArrayList (With Values): ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(10, 20, 30));
• List Interface (Best Practice): List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();

Feature                 	Standard Array (int[])	                                  ArrayList (ArrayList<Integer>)
Size      	              Fixed once created	                                     Dynamic (grows automatically)
Data Type	                Holds primitives (int)	                                 Holds objects (Integer)
Adding Items	            arr[0] = 10;	                                           result.add(10);
Getting Items	            arr[0]	                                                 result.get(0)
Checking Length	          arr.length	                                             result.size()

                                                          **************** STRING ***************
String is a sequence of characters used to store text or words in java.
-> String is an object of the `string` class in java that represents a sequence of characters.
>>>> DECLARATION
 String name="KOUSHIK";

1.length()
returns the number of characters in a string

2.tolowercase()
converts all characters into lowercase
String s="JAVA";
System.out.println(s.tolowerCase());
output=java

3.equals()
compares two strings based on their content.
String a="java";
string b="java";
System.out.println(a.equals(b));

4.equalsIgnoreCase()
compares strings without considering uppercase or lowercase differences
String  a="JAVA"
String b="java"
System.out.println(a.equalsIgnoreCase(b));

5.contains()
checks whether a String contains a particular sequence of characters.
String s="Java program";
System.out.print(s.contains("java"));
output=true

6.replace()

replaces characters or character sequences
String s="java";
System.out.println(s.replace('a','o'));
output:jovo

7.trim()
removes leading and trailing spaces
String s="  Hello  "
System.out.println(s.trim());
output=Hello
